{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend (higher highs / higher lows), add an upward level shift to the whole path (roughly +2 to +5 €/t), increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to regime changes; if the most recent close breaks above the 20–30d range/mean, tilt the trajectory upward (increase daily slope) rather than keeping it flat/mean-reverting.",
    "Horizon-dependent underpricing: the underestimation grows with lead time (largest errors around weeks 2–4). Apply a larger correction after day ~7 (e.g., +1 €/t week1, +2–3 €/t week2, +3–5 €/t weeks3–4 in strong uptrends).",
    "Excess mean reversion: model repeatedly pulls prices back toward mid-60s even when outcomes stay elevated (high-60s). Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility is rising and the 10d average > 30d average.",
    "Post-spike behavior: after an upside break, the model often predicts a dip/flattening that doesn’t occur. Replace predicted pullbacks with consolidation near the new level (keep prices within ~0 to -1 €/t of the new plateau rather than -3 to -6 €/t)."
  ]
}
